# Names implemented twice: once as a C operator and once in PostScript.
#
# One of the two is in force and the other is unreachable, and which one
# it is settled while the language was read -- by the order the
# dictionaries were filled and searched. Nothing announces the choice, and
# the wrong one still answers: definefont bound against the C findfont
# registers the font and then returns a substitute face, so a program's
# own font is quietly replaced by another with the same operators and
# different glyphs.
#
# So the choice is written down. One "name winner" per line.
#
#   postscript  the PostScript implementation is in force. It is wrapped,
#               which is how it is recognised at run time: the wrapping
#               pass found a procedure under the name, promoted it, and
#               recorded it in the register of wrapped procedures.
#
#   c           the C operator is in force and the PostScript body is
#               unreachable. No name is in this state now. The category
#               stands because the state is reachable and silent: a boot
#               file defining a name the C side already installs, on a
#               path the normal flow does not take, is a body nothing
#               runs and nothing reports. breakhere was the one such
#               pair until its PostScript body -- a shim in the fallback
#               block graphics.ps takes only when loaded standalone --
#               was removed as unreachable.
#
# A name implemented twice and not listed here fails the check: it is a
# choice nobody made on purpose.

ashow       postscript
awidthshow  postscript
findfont    postscript
setfont     postscript
show        postscript
stringwidth postscript
widthshow   postscript
